  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "On the morning of the wedding, Kostya and Denis wake up with terrible hangovers and zero memory of the night before—until Kostya spots Katya's white wedding dress hanging in the room! Denis grabs the dress to return it before Katya notices.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя, скажи, это сейчас ты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Кажется, мы вчера сильно перебрали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я вообще ничего не помню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "У меня чуть сердце не встало!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты всё-таки спёр свадебное платье!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, неужели это сделал я?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты хоть что-нибудь сейчас помнишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы вчера ночью были в отеле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Откуда мы взяли ключ от номера?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Этот ключ был у меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Надо срочно вернуть его назад.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Быстро верни свадебное платье Кате!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя, скажи, это сейчас ты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostya, tell me, is that you right now?' Denis addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Кажется, мы вчера сильно перебрали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It seems we drank way too much yesterday.' The meaning hinges on 'it seems' working together with 'drank too much' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я вообще ничего не помню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not remember anything at all.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'remember' to negate the statement. Kostya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "У меня чуть сердце не встало!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My heart nearly stopped!' The grammar packs 'at' and 'to stand up' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты всё-таки спёр свадебное платье!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You stole the wedding dress after all!' Kostya addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'a dress'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, неужели это сделал я?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, did I really do that?' The meaning hinges on 'wait' working together with 'I' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты хоть что-нибудь сейчас помнишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you remember anything at all right now?' Denis addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'remember'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы вчера ночью были в отеле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Last night we were at the hotel.' The grammar packs 'we' and 'hotel' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Откуда мы взяли ключ от номера?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where did we get the room key from?' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'took' covers a group or the polite 'you'. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Этот ключ был у меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I had that key with me.' The meaning hinges on 'this' working together with 'me' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Надо срочно вернуть его назад.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need to return it back urgently.' Kostya brings out the contrast between 'need' and 'urgently' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Быстро верни свадебное платье Кате!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Quickly return the wedding dress to Katya!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'return'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
